      "created_at": "2018-09-18T07:27:49Z",
      "updated_at": "2018-09-18T07:27:49Z",
      "body": "This is the link to the official site of the European Union: [European Commission - Legal Grounds](https://ec.europa.eu/info/law/law-topic/data-protection/reform/rules-business-and-organisations/legal-grounds-processing-data/grounds-processing) \r\n\r\nIn the section \"When is consent valid?\" there are some requirements on how to have a valid consent which are:\r\n\r\n1. it must be **freely given;**\r\n2. it must be **informed**;\r\n3. it must be given for a **specific purpose**;\r\n4. all the reasons for the processing must be clearly stated;\r\n5. it is **explicit** and given via a positive act (for example an electronic tick-box that the individual has to explicitly check online or a signature on a form);\r\n6. it **uses clear and plain language** and is clearly visible;\r\n7. it is possible to **withdraw consent** and that fact is explained (for example an unsubscribe link at the end of an electronic newsletter email).\r\n\r\nBullet points 2 - 7 have to be covered by the organisations data privacy statement, but maybe a default statement can be provided, i.e. which personal data is collected (mail, name, optional phone number etc, maybe IP/browser at login?!). As I told, we are already working on a data privacy statement and I'm happy to contribute it.\r\nBullet point 1 addresses the actual implementation. As mentioned, specifically activating a box or clicking a button should be sufficient. But I'll ask again whether double opt-in is required or rather a nice to have.",
      "author_association": "NONE",
      "pin": null,
